{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high vs. realized in the first ~1–15 days (often +0.2 to +1.5), but become materially low in the back half (~day 20–30) when the market trends up (often -2 to -7). Apply a tilt that increases with horizon (small downward adjustment early, increasing upward adjustment late).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: during strong upward regimes the forecast path is too flat and fails to accelerate, missing late-stage rallies (e.g., realized jumps into 71–76 while forecast stays ~66–70). Add a momentum/trend term based on last 5–10 day slope that compounds with horizon.",
    "Post-spike mean-reversion overshoot: when recent prices spike, the model tends to project continued elevated levels and/or slow decay, while realized prices often dip more in the next ~10–20 days. After a sharp up-move in history, damp the next 1–15 day forecast (stronger damping if last 3–5 day return is large).",
    "Trough underestimation: around local lows (~64–66), the model is too pessimistic and misses rebounds (realized recovers faster/higher). When recent history shows a drawdown followed by stabilization, reduce mean-reversion strength and lift the next 5–15 day path.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast range is narrower than realized (misses both late upside and some mid-horizon dips). Inflate deviations from the forecast mean with horizon (wider bands / larger step sizes), especially beyond day ~20."
  ]
}
